# Application layer (MediatR)

## Feature layout

```
Features/{RoleOrArea}/{Entity}/Commands|Queries/{Name}/
  {Name}Command|Query.cs
  {Name}Command|QueryHandler.cs
  {Name}Command|QueryValidator.cs   # when needed
```

DTOs live in the feature's `Dtos/` folder (or shared feature Dtos).

## Commands / queries

- Use `sealed record` for requests implementing `IRequest<Result>` or `IRequest<Result<T>>`.
- Handlers: `sealed class` + primary constructor, implement `IRequestHandler<,>`.
- Pass `UserId` (and similar) on the command/query from the controller — do not read HTTP context here.
- Return `Result` / `Result<T>` (`Success`, `Failure`, `NotFound`, `Conflict`) — never throw for expected business failures.
- Inject `IApplicationDbContext` and `IRequestLanguage` when needed; use EF via the contract, not concrete DbContext.

```csharp
// ✅
public sealed record CreateLessonCommand(...) : IRequest<Result<LessonDto>>;

public sealed class CreateLessonCommandHandler(IApplicationDbContext db, IRequestLanguage lang)
    : IRequestHandler<CreateLessonCommand, Result<LessonDto>>
{
    public async Task<Result<LessonDto>> Handle(...) { /* ... */ return Result<LessonDto>.Success(dto); }
}
```

## Validation

- FluentValidation `AbstractValidator<T>` next to the command/query.
- Keep field rules in validators; keep domain/authorization checks in handlers.
